<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Macro Calculator for Strength Athletes</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by our editorial team. Updated May 2026.</em></p>

  <p>Calculate daily protein, carbohydrate, and fat targets based on your bodyweight, training intensity, and goal. This calculator is built specifically for strength athletes rather than general weight-loss applications — the protein and carbohydrate targets reflect the demands of high-intensity resistance training, not aerobic conditioning or sedentary maintenance.</p>

  <h2>Calculate your macros</h2>
  <p><em>The interactive calculator will load on first publish.</em></p>

  <h2>How we set macro targets</h2>
  <p>The macro targets generated by this calculator are based on current evidence-based recommendations from sports nutrition research, primarily the position statements of the International Society of Sports Nutrition (ISSN) and Precision Nutrition's applied framework for strength athlete fueling. Here's how each macro is calculated:</p>

  <h3>Protein</h3>
  <p>Protein targets are set at 0.7–1.0 g per pound of bodyweight (1.6–2.2 g/kg), scaled upward based on training intensity and goal phase. The research basis for this range: a 2017 meta-analysis by Morton et al. in the British Journal of Sports Medicine analyzed 49 studies and found that protein intakes above 0.82 g/lb (1.62 g/kg) produced no additional gains in muscle mass or strength in the average lifter. However, the upper range of the recommendation (1.0 g/lb) is supported for athletes in caloric deficit (where higher protein protects lean mass), athletes with very high training volumes, and those in the early stages of a new training phase where muscle protein synthesis is elevated.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Maintenance or bulk phase, moderate training (3–4 days/week):</strong> 0.7–0.8 g/lb</li>
    <li><strong>Maintenance or bulk phase, high training volume (5+ days/week):</strong> 0.8–0.9 g/lb</li>
    <li><strong>Deficit/cut phase:</strong> 0.9–1.0 g/lb to protect lean mass</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Protein target is calculated first because it is the highest nutritional priority for strength athletes and the most clearly established by research.</p>

  <h3>Fat</h3>
  <p>Fat targets are set at a minimum of 0.3–0.5 g/lb bodyweight (0.7–1.1 g/kg) with a floor of 50 g per day regardless of bodyweight. The lower bound matters: fat intake below 15–20% of total calories is associated with disruptions to testosterone production and sex hormone synthesis, which directly affects recovery and adaptation in strength athletes. Fat is not a performance nutrient in the direct sense that protein and carbohydrates are, but chronically low fat intake impairs the hormonal environment necessary for strength development. The upper bound is flexible — fat intakes above the minimum are adjusted based on total calorie targets and carbohydrate priorities.</p>

  <h3>Carbohydrate</h3>
  <p>Carbohydrates fill the remaining calories after protein and fat targets are set. This is appropriate because carbohydrates are the primary fuel source for high-intensity anaerobic exercise (including all compound barbell work), and their quantity should be determined by what's left after the non-negotiable protein and fat minimums are met. For a lifter in a caloric surplus, carbohydrate intake is typically high. For a lifter in a deficit, carbohydrates compress to create the caloric gap. Spreading carbohydrates around training — larger portions before and after sessions — is a common timing strategy that supports performance and recovery without requiring precise gram-by-gram tracking throughout the day.</p>

  <h2>Calorie targets by goal phase</h2>
  <p>The calorie target sets the outer boundary within which macros are distributed. Calorie targets are estimated from your Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE), which is calculated from your bodyweight and activity level:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Bulk (muscle gain):</strong> +250 to +500 calories per day above TDEE. A conservative bulk (+250) minimizes fat gain; an aggressive bulk (+500) accelerates muscle gain but adds more body fat alongside it. Most natural lifters achieve better body composition results on slower, controlled bulks.</li>
    <li><strong>Maintain:</strong> At TDEE. Used during competition prep maintenance phases, transition periods between cutting and bulking, or when body composition is near goal and the priority is sustaining strength gains.</li>
    <li><strong>Cut (fat loss):</strong> −500 to −750 calories per day below TDEE. The 500-calorie deficit is a commonly cited starting point (~1 lb/week loss rate); the 750-calorie target is used for faster cuts where timeline is constrained. Deficits above 1,000 calories per day tend to accelerate muscle loss and compromise training performance significantly for strength athletes.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These are starting estimates, not precise prescriptions. The only reliable feedback mechanism for calorie targets is your bodyweight trend over 2–3 weeks. If the scale is not moving in the expected direction — gaining weight on a bulk, losing weight on a cut — adjust total calories by 10–15% and reassess. Calculators estimate TDEE; your actual metabolic rate varies based on factors no formula can fully capture.</p>

  <h2>Timing and meal structure</h2>
  <p>For strength athletes, three practical timing principles outweigh specific meal timing requirements:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Don't train fasted for heavy sessions.</strong> Some carbohydrate intake before training (1–2 hours out) supports peak performance on heavy compound work. Total fasting before max-effort squats or deadlifts is an unnecessary performance handicap.</li>
    <li><strong>Protein within a few hours of training.</strong> The "anabolic window" is less narrow than once thought, but getting a protein-containing meal or shake within 2–3 hours of a training session supports muscle protein synthesis timing.</li>
    <li><strong>Distribute protein across meals.</strong> Research supports distributing daily protein across 3–4 meals of approximately 30–50 g rather than concentrating it in one or two large meals. Each meal triggers a protein synthesis response; spreading intake takes advantage of multiple stimulation events.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Adjusting over time</h2>
  <p>Track your bodyweight and training performance together — not just one in isolation. On a bulk, the goal is to see bodyweight trending up (0.5–1.0 lb/week for most natural lifters) with training performance also improving. If weight is going up but strength is stagnant, the issue is likely training, not nutrition. If strength is going up but weight isn't, you may be in a smaller surplus than the calculator suggests — not a problem unless you want to gain mass faster. On a cut, the goal is to lose weight while maintaining training performance as closely as possible. If strength is declining rapidly early in a cut, you may be in too aggressive a deficit or insufficiently protecting protein intake.</p>

  <p>Return to the <a href="/">1RM calculator</a>, see the <a href="/powerlifting-total-calculator">powerlifting total calculator</a>, or read our <a href="/guides">training guides</a>.</p>
</BaseLayout>
